#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ytdl — download audio or video from a link, and say what it is doing.

Not just YouTube. yt-dlp handles about 1,750 sites — Bandcamp, SoundCloud,
Vimeo, the BBC, archive.org, Mixcloud — and falls back to a generic attempt on
any plain media link. `ytdl --sites bandcamp` checks one.

yt-dlp does the downloading. This exists for the talking: yt-dlp's own progress
bar rewrites one terminal line many times a second, so a screen reader spends
the whole download interrupting itself and never finishes a sentence. Here you
get one line per event and silence in between.

    ytdl <url>              your default format, into your default folder
    ytdl <url> <url> <url>  several at once, one after another
    ytdl                    take the URL from the clipboard
    ytdl <url> --info       title, length, what you would get — no download
    ytdl --jobs             what is downloading now, what finished recently

  choosing a format
    --format KIND           best, m4a, opus, mp3, wav, flac or video
    --video --mp3 --wav     shorthand for the same
    --pick                  list the real formats and choose by number
    --list-formats          list them and stop
    --max-height 720        cap video height

  part of a video
    --from 1:30             start there
    --to-time 3:45          stop there
    --chapters              also split into one file per chapter
    --subtitles             also fetch English subtitles as .srt

  playlists
    --playlist              the whole list, into its own numbered folder
    --items 1-10            or 3,5,7 — just those
    --no-resume             re-fetch items already downloaded

  settings
    --setup                 walk through every setting, one at a time
    --settings              show what is set
    --set notify=yes        change one setting without the questions
    --set-default wav       shorthand for --set format=wav

  several at once
    --batch links.txt       a file of links, one per line
    --jobs                  what is downloading now, and what just finished

  anything this does not cover
    --yt-dlp="--flag val"   raw arguments handed straight to yt-dlp
    --yt-dlp-help           every yt-dlp option there is

  everything else
    --sites bandcamp        is a site supported? (about 1,750 are)
    --to PATH               a different folder, just this once
    --name "..."            set the filename yourself
    --progress              a percentage line every 10 seconds, no faster
    --cookies [BROWSER]     sign-in cookies, for "confirm you're not a bot"
    --notify                Pushover ping on finish and on failure
    --quiet                 print the final path and nothing else
    --update                brew upgrade yt-dlp now, and say what changed
    --no-auto-update        never upgrade yt-dlp on its own

Nothing here ever opens a file. Downloads are written and left alone, so
whatever app owns the format on your Mac is never launched.

A playlist — a YouTube list, a Bandcamp album, a SoundCloud set — goes into
its own folder, numbered in order, and keeps a small archive file beside the
files so re-running it resumes rather than starting over. One bad item does not
abandon the rest.

yt-dlp keeps itself current without being asked. It upgrades when a download
fails and a newer release exists — the moment it actually matters — and again
at most once a day after a *successful* download, so the upgrade has usually
already happened before a break can reach you. Neither check ever runs before
a download: nothing stands between you and the file you asked for.

--format best never re-encodes: the source codec is kept and only the
container changes, so AAC lands as .m4a and Opus as .opus. Naming a specific
format instead (m4a, mp3, wav, flac) will convert when the source is not
already that codec — which is a lossy generation for the lossy ones. That is
the trade: `best` guarantees no quality loss, a named format guarantees the
file type.

Downloads land wherever --set-default --to puts them. Part-files never do —
they go to a local scratch directory, so half a download is never synced to
your phone and then deleted.

Several links run one after another rather than at once, which keeps sites from
rate-limiting you, and **one failure never abandons the rest** — the same rule
playlists follow. The run reports how many of them made it.

yt-dlp has roughly 200 options. Wrapping each one would make this help
unreadable by ear, which is the problem the tool exists to solve, so instead
--yt-dlp passes anything straight through and --yt-dlp-help lists the lot. That
is full coverage without the bloat.
"""

